{"version":"ai-mcq-bank-v2-la-batch15-high-quality-rewritten-affected-2026-05-22-stem-fixed-inequality-rendering-fixed-student-facing-qc-2026-05-31-pt-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch2-foundations-counting-2026-06-11-pt-batch3-counting-probability-rules-2026-06-11-pt-batch4-conditioning-2026-06-11-pt-batch5-random-variables-2026-06-11-pt-batch6-discrete-distributions-2026-06-11-pt-batch7-distribution-foundations-2026-06-11-pt-batch8-continuous-expectation-2026-06-11-pt-batch9-non-week7-expectation-transform-2026-06-11-pt-batch10-non-week7-process-summary-2026-06-11-pt-batch11-non-week7-final-summary-shape-2026-06-11-quality-round1-2026-06-14-quality-round2-2026-06-14-quality-round3-2026-06-14-quality-round4-deep-2026-06-14+quality-round5-difficulty-2026-06-14+quality-round6-expression-2026-06-14+quality-round7-answer-cue-2026-06-14+quality-round8-answer-shape-2026-06-14+quality-round9-answer-position-2026-06-14+quality-round10-template-padding-2026-06-14+quality-round11-generic-stems-2026-06-14+quality-round12-deterministic-cleanup-2026-06-15+quality-pilot-deep-rework-2026-06-15","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/gradient.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/gradient.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For a real-valued function $f\\colon\\boldsymbol{E}\\to\\mathbb{R}$ with $\\boldsymbol{E}\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n$, what is $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$?","options":["The matrix of second partial derivatives at $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$.","The value $f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$ itself.","The column vector of first partial derivatives at $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$.","The set of all points where $f$ is zero."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"03d23e934809364703ac5adc"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What condition on $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is included for the gradient?","options":["$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is a boundary point of $\\boldsymbol{E}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is outside $\\boldsymbol{E}$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is the maximum point of $f$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is an interior point of $\\boldsymbol{E}$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"58141a4d44adea7c21e4c0ff"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_003","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$, what is $\\nabla f(x,y)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix} x e^{x+y} \\\\ y e^{x+y} \\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix} e^{x+y} \\\\ e^{x+y} \\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix} e^x \\\\ e^y \\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix} x+y \\\\ e^{x+y} \\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3e491bccf552de4a063051df"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = x^2y+y^3$, what is $\\nabla f(x,y)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix} x^2 \\\\ 3y^2 \\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix} 2x+y \\\\ x^2+y^2 \\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix} 2xy \\\\ x^2+3y^2 \\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix} y^2 \\\\ 2xy+3y^2 \\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6105131e236a8d7c5da9649e"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)\\cdot\\boldsymbol{v}$ represent when $\\boldsymbol{v}$ is a unit vector?","options":["The value $f(\\boldsymbol{v})$.","The Hessian matrix at $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$.","The distance from $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ to $\\boldsymbol{v}$.","The directional derivative $\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c690fe25bce385d17366183a"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is said when $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = \\boldsymbol{0}$?","options":["$f$ is vector-valued at $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is not in the domain.","Every directional derivative must be positive.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is a stationary point of $f$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"81693ea3d645e90d48cad25d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = \\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\boldsymbol{v} = \\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$. What is $\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$?","options":["$-1$","$2$","$1$","$3$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7afe12f18de21da38f0434a5"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = x^2y+y^3$, what is $\\nabla f(1,1)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"0a27c81951bc7dd2a8c694e4"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the definition require $f$ to be real-valued?","options":["Because vector-valued functions cannot have any derivatives","Because real-valued functions are always linear, which makes the gradient well-defined","Because the gradient listed is the vector of partial derivatives of a scalar-valued function.","Because $\\nabla f$ is defined only when $n = 1$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"3bc80075ade3a283136fcfd0"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If $\\nabla f(x_0)\\ne0$, which directional-derivative claim is justified?","options":["Every direction has the same directional derivative.","The largest directional derivative occurs in the gradient direction.","The gradient is tangent to the level set at $x_0$.","The largest decrease occurs in the gradient direction."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bfe3b9db377d92902f32c6b1"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0) = \\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\boldsymbol{v} = \\begin{pmatrix}3/5\\\\4/5\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$?","options":["$7$","$1$","$5$","$25$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"2fb25015a8b3d3b024c2d5ef"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y) = e^{x+y}$, at which point below is the gradient equal to $\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$?","options":["$(0,0)$","$(1,0)$","$(0,1)$","$(1,1)$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"32f8800a6f733586e8f045c5"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"How many components does $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$ have for $f:E\\subseteq\\mathbb{R}^n\\to\\mathbb{R}$?","options":["$1$ component in every case.","$k$ components for arbitrary codomain dimension $k$.","$n$ components.","No components."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"faa14d549aee1909254e4f79"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which derivatives form the entries of the gradient?","options":["The second partial derivatives only.","The values of $f$ at all points.","The directional derivatives in every possible direction as separate entries.","The first partial derivatives of $f$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"8de50ddc25cb6431b516d9f3"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)=\\boldsymbol{0}$ indicate in this terminology?","options":["$f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)=0$ necessarily.","$f$ is not differentiable anywhere.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is a stationary point of $f$.","$\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ is outside the domain."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6dc4e2d83286c4537a5b99b4"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x+y$, what is $\\nabla f(x,y)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}x\\\\y\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}y\\\\x\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e3383a1bfd77861004ffe95d"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x,y)=x^2+3y$, what is $\\nabla f(2,1)$?","options":["$\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c9e43e05ff4d2e619fad2c79"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $\\boldsymbol{x}_0$ called?","options":["A unit vector.","A compact set.","A stationary point.","A preimage."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c911a89a9ab6c6f81f974ad8"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the relation between the gradient and directional derivatives?","options":["The gradient is unrelated to directional derivatives.","The gradient is always equal to every unit vector.","The directional derivative is the norm of the input point","The gradient dotted with a unit vector gives the directional derivative"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"bfb7c8b43feed9f2bfd23e40"},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_gradient_13fb2e17_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"If $\\nabla f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\boldsymbol{v}=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$, what is $\\mathrm{D}_{\\boldsymbol{v}}f(\\boldsymbol{x}_0)$?","options":["$1$","$3$","$0$","$2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"38382fd28de7ccad7500053a"}]}}